From 50710a44a987234f3722381b11157c517afc2b04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 06:39:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Stop the video model walking a second person into a solo shot
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Measured on job #51878's base video. Coverage worked: 0-3s is one speaker
alone, 3-6s the other alone, chest up, clean. The keyframes are right. Then
around 4.9s a dark shape enters frame left, and by 5.5s a large
out-of-focus body fills the foreground of a shot that is supposed to hold
exactly one face. That is what LatentSync refused, twice, on the same
scene — not a transient provider failure.

Guided scenes animate through animateBrollStills, which builds

    `${visual}. ${motion}`

the same construction the character path was fixed for. Guided never got
that fix, so a lip-synced shot hands the video model its full coverage
direction — including "eyes toward the person they are speaking with just
off-frame" and "Do not include any other person". That tells the model
someone IS off-frame, then names them in a negative, and diffusion models
read a named negative as an invitation. Give it five seconds to fill and it
brings that person in.

A shot the finishing pass will sync now animates from the hold instruction
instead: no scene prose, no negative naming anyone. The hold also now
states what the frame DOES contain — the same single subject first frame to
last, nothing new entering — rather than who must stay out of it, which is
the phrasing that backfired.

Ordinary AI b-roll keeps its prose byte-for-byte. Most of those scenes have
no person in them, so the character-shot language would be noise; a test
that pins the existing b-roll prompt caught that over-reach and was right
to.

Which shots the pass will sync is known at enqueue, so the runner passes
those scene ids down and the animation stage decides per scene.

